    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 what this measures. Evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>India has no single statistician for its states. Each state's Directorate of Economics and Statistics estimates its own domestic product, following methods the National Statistical Office sets, and sends the figures to MoSPI, which publishes them together. Some items cannot be measured inside one state, such as railways, banking, communications and central government, so the national totals for those are shared out between states by formulas based on things like deposits, employees or traffic.</p>
      <p>Every figure here is on the 2011-12 base: values at constant prices are in 2011-12 rupees, which is how growth after inflation is measured. India's national accounts moved to a 2022-23 base in 2026. The state series have not moved yet, so state figures cannot be added up and compared with the new national GDP.</p>
      <p>States publish on their own timetables. The latest year is usually an advance or quick estimate and is revised, sometimes by several per cent, as tax, crop and company data arrive. MoSPI does not label which years are final for which state, and neither do we know. Andhra Pradesh and Telangana are shown separately from 2011-12, as each state now estimates its own past. Jammu and Kashmir is shown as two series because its territory changed in October 2019.</p>
      <p>The RBI's Handbook of Statistics on Indian States reprints these figures with a lag of about a year and adds state finances, farm output, power, roads, banking, health and prices. Its domestic product tables match MoSPI's exactly up to 2020-21; for later years the Handbook carries an older round of revisions. This guide draws on MoSPI for the economy and on the Handbook for state finances, and bakes every Handbook table into its data.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 misreadings. Evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"People in Delhi earn seven times what people in Bihar earn."</h3><p>Per-capita product counts what is produced in a place, divided by the people who live there. Lakhs of people produce inside Delhi, Goa or Chandigarh but live outside, so those figures are inflated; states that send workers away, like Bihar, produce less than their people earn, because remittances from Mumbai or Dubai are counted where the work is done. Household spending surveys, in the consumption guide, show a much smaller gap.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"Sikkim is the richest state, so Sikkimese are the richest Indians."</h3><p>Sikkim's figure is large production from hydropower and pharmaceutical plants divided by a small population. Much of the profit belongs to companies based elsewhere. A high per-capita product in a small state can describe a few large plants more than the lives of its people.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"The states add up to India's GDP."</h3><p>They do not, quite. Some states have not published the latest year, the state figures are on the 2011-12 base while national GDP has moved to 2022-23, and states revise on their own timetables. Compare states with each other, not with a sum.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"This state grew 12% last year."</h3><p>Check whether that is at current or constant prices. A state's economy measured in the rupees of each year grows with prices as well as output. Real growth, at constant prices, is the one to compare. And the latest year is an estimate that will be revised.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"Poor states are catching up, so the problem is solving itself."</h3><p>The multiple between the richest and poorest has narrowed, but the gap in rupees has kept growing, and at recent rates it would take decades for the poorest states to reach today's middle.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- § 12 every Handbook table. Inputs: data/coverage/states.hbs-index.json and the states-hbs-* bundles -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">12</span><h2>Every table of the RBI Handbook</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{hbsRows.length} files, as published</span></div>
    <div class="prose"><p>The RBI's Handbook of Statistics on Indian States has {new Set(hbsRows.map((r) => r.table)).size} tables, from birth rates and forest cover to bank credit and state debt. Every one is here as published, one file per table (the domestic product tables carry the old 2004-05 base as a separate file), with each state a column, undivided Andhra Pradesh and Jammu and Kashmir before and after 2019 kept apart. Values are as the RBI serves them: rupee amounts are in rupees.</p></div>
    <div class="break hbs-list">
