  <footer class="provenance">
    <h2 class="prov-h">Sources, method and revisions</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Sources of record</h6>
        <p>MoSPI, National Accounts Statistics, regional accounts (eSankhyiki, indicators 23 to 34, base 2011-12), estimates by each state's Directorate of Economics and Statistics. RBI, Handbook of Statistics on Indian States, all 182 tables, retrieved from the RBI's Database on Indian Economy through the owner's local mirror. Data vintage <b id="prov-vintage">–</b>.</p>
        <p>Checked by hand: the Handbook's Table 19 gives Bihar ₹69,321 per person for 2024-25, as baked; MoSPI's newer round gives ₹69,211.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Method</h6>
        <p>Growth is computed from levels and checked against MoSPI's own one-decimal rates. GSDP for Assam, Haryana, Nagaland and Ladakh, which MoSPI's GSDP table omits, is built from MoSPI's published value added, taxes and subsidies, the identity that reproduces every published state. Shares, ratios, the panel of states and correlations are India Atlas computations; nothing is estimated or filled.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Breaks and defects</h6>
        <p>Jammu and Kashmir is split at 2019-20; undivided Andhra Pradesh in the Handbook's older tables is a separate series. Two MoSPI defects are left out: Karnataka's net value added, served as a copy of its gross value added, and Karnataka's figure under indicator 32, a copy of per-capita GSDP. The Handbook's end-March 2015 debt for Delhi, Puducherry, Tripura and Uttarakhand, several times the years either side, is left out of the debt ratios.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>This guide</h6>
        <p>Version 1.0, built October 2026. Revised when MoSPI publishes new state estimates and when the RBI publishes the next Handbook.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
